# Which Cloudflare account this project deploys to — per project, not per machine.
#
# Copy to `.env.cloudflare` (gitignored by the `.env*` rule) and paste an API
# token scoped to THIS project's account only.
#
# Why a token per project instead of `wrangler login`: one OAuth session points
# at one account, so several accounts on one machine means logging out and back
# in to switch — and a deploy that lands in the wrong account is silent and
# succeeds. A token here plus `account_id` in wrangler.jsonc makes the target
# a property of the repo. They also cross-check: a token for the wrong account
# fails against the pinned id instead of publishing somewhere unexpected.
#
# NOT named `.env`: Next loads `.env` and `.env.local` automatically, which
# would put this token in the app's build and server environment for no reason.
# Next does not load `.env.cloudflare`, and wrangler reads it via --env-file.
#
# Make one at: dash.cloudflare.com -> My Profile -> API Tokens -> Create Token
#   template:          "Edit Cloudflare Workers"
#   Account Resources: Include -> Capytools          (that one account, nothing else)
#   Zone Resources:    Include -> capytools.app      (needed for the custom domain)
# Running wrangler by hand: --env-file must come AFTER the subcommand, and it
# takes a list, so anything following it is read as another filename.
#   works:  wrangler secret put GITHUB_TOKEN --env-file .env.cloudflare
#   broken: wrangler --env-file .env.cloudflare secret put GITHUB_TOKEN
# `npm run deploy` and `npm run cf-typegen` already place it correctly.
CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN=
